6. Never weaken RLS, bypass authorization, or grant broad public permissions to make functionality work.
7. Never simulate successful persistence when a database operation fails.
8. Never use mock records, static business data, or localStorage as an authoritative production data source.
9. Do not claim that an external integration works unless it has been genuinely tested.
10. Preserve backward compatibility unless a necessary change is documented and validated.
11. Do not introduce new dependencies unless there is a clear technical justification.
12. Use migrations for required schema changes, preserve existing data, and document dependencies and rollback considerations.
13. Use isolated staging data for testing wherever possible.
14. If an integration, credential, or external account is unavailable, implement the safe portions and document what remains blocked.

