5. STAGE B — COMPLETE ADMIN MODULE FUNCTIONALITY

Audit every existing admin module and implement the functionality appropriate to its actual purpose.

The following areas must be covered wherever corresponding modules exist.

A. Dashboard and business overview

- Calculate dashboard metrics from authoritative persisted records.
- Ensure revenue, booking counts, pending inquiries, upcoming departures, and other figures use clearly defined business rules.
- Distinguish confirmed, pending, cancelled, refunded, and failed transactions where applicable.
- Apply appropriate date ranges and timezone handling.
- Remove fabricated totals and misleading sample statistics.
- Provide useful loading, empty, and error states.
- Ensure the dashboard does not count the same transaction multiple times.

Do not label bookings as paid merely because a booking exists.

B. Tours and catalogue management

- Create, edit, publish, unpublish, archive, duplicate, and delete tours where permitted.
- Validate slugs, identifiers, required fields, prices, durations, destinations, and categories.
- Preserve referential integrity when modifying or removing related records.
- Manage images, galleries, itinerary stops, highlights, inclusions, exclusions, and FAQs.
- Ensure tour changes appear on the public website from persisted data.
- Ensure unpublished and archived tours are excluded from public queries.
- Prevent duplicate submissions and misleading success notifications.

Use existing services and database structures where possible.

C. Destinations and categories

- Complete CRUD operations supported by the existing application.
- Validate relationships and prevent invalid orphaned records.
- Maintain accurate counts derived from published tours.
- Ensure category and destination changes update public filters and navigation.
- Prevent deletion of referenced records unless a safe, explicit business rule permits it.

D. Bookings and reservations

Implement and verify the complete booking lifecycle supported by the existing product:

- List, search, filter, and inspect bookings.
- View guest details with appropriate authorization.
- Assign booking references safely.
- Update booking status using valid state transitions.
- Manage booking notes and operational details.
- Track payment status separately from booking status.
- Support cancellations and refunds only through valid business workflows.
- Record changes in an audit trail.
- Prevent accidental duplicate booking creation.
- Ensure the customer portal reflects legitimate booking changes.

Define valid booking and payment state transitions. Prevent invalid transitions, such as marking an unpaid booking as paid without verified payment evidence.

Do not invent a new workflow that conflicts with the existing schema. Document and safely migrate only when necessary.

E. Availability and departures

- Manage departure dates, capacity, blackout dates, and availability.
- Prevent negative capacity and invalid date ranges.
- Keep public availability consistent with operational records.
- Validate capacity at booking time, not only when rendering the frontend.
- Prevent overselling when simultaneous bookings compete for the same capacity.
- Use appropriate database transactions, locking, or atomic RPC operations when needed.
- Make repeated requests safe where idempotency is required.

F. Pickup locations and extras

- Complete creation, editing, activation, deactivation, and deletion where appropriate.
- Validate pricing type and surcharge values.
- Ensure inactive records are excluded from customer checkout.
- Ensure changes are reflected in booking calculations.
- Preserve the exact pricing distinction between per-person and per-booking fees.
- Prevent duplicate surcharge application.

G. Reviews and moderation

- List and inspect submitted reviews.
- Publish, unpublish, and delete reviews with authorization.
- Preserve moderation history where appropriate.
- Calculate ratings and distributions from genuine persisted review records.
- Prevent unpublished reviews from appearing publicly.
- Prevent unauthorized users from editing another customer's review.
- Verify that review submission and moderation failures are surfaced honestly.

H. Customer relationship management

Audit and complete existing CRM functionality, including:

- Customer records and profiles.
- Customer booking history.
- Inquiries and lead management.
- Lead status, assignment, notes, and follow-up dates.
- Customer communication history where implemented.
- Search, filters, pagination, and relevant customer segmentation.
- Customer profile updates and deduplication where supported.

Protect personally identifiable information through least-privilege access. Do not expose private customer records through public queries.

I. Communications

Inspect existing email, WhatsApp, notification, and messaging modules.

For each integration:

- Validate required configuration.
- Keep credentials on the server.
- Validate destinations and message content.
- Record delivery status where supported.
- Handle provider timeouts and API errors.
- Avoid duplicate messages during retries.
- Keep delivery failures separate from booking success.
- Ensure the user interface accurately reports whether a message was queued, sent, delivered, or failed, according to what the provider actually confirms.

Do not claim delivery when the provider merely accepted a request.

Do not send real messages during automated testing without an explicitly configured sandbox or test mode.

J. Finance and payments

Audit all existing financial modules and complete supported workflows:

- Payment records and reconciliation.
- Booking totals and outstanding balances.
- Refund tracking.
- Revenue reports.
- Currency handling.
- Financial adjustments and audit history.
- Transaction references and payment-provider integration.

Use integer minor units or an appropriate decimal-safe approach for financial calculations. Avoid floating-point rounding errors in persisted monetary values.

Define how EUR, USD, GBP, and EGP are represented, converted, rounded, and reported. Do not silently combine amounts from different currencies into one revenue figure.

A payment must be verified using trusted provider evidence or an authorized reconciliation process—not a client-supplied status.

Do not create fake transactions or initiate real charges during testing.

K. Suppliers, vessels, guides, and operations

Where these modules exist:

- Complete management of vessels, guides, operational assignments, and supplier records.
- Validate capacity, assignment dates, availability, and conflicts.
- Prevent invalid or conflicting assignments.
- Connect operational assignments to the appropriate bookings and departures.
- Preserve existing schema naming and relationships.
- Verify that operational changes are reflected in related views.

L. Reports, analytics, and exports

- Calculate reports from actual records.
- Define each metric and its underlying query.
- Apply date ranges and timezone boundaries correctly.
- Avoid double-counting bookings, payments, or customers.
- Apply role-based access to financial and personal data.
- Validate export filters against the visible report.
- Avoid leaking private information through generated files.
- Handle empty datasets and large result sets correctly.

M. Settings, users, and access control

- Inspect all existing configuration and access-management modules.
- Protect admin provisioning and role changes.
- Ensure ordinary users cannot grant themselves administrative privileges.
- Apply least-privilege authorization to sensitive actions.
- Validate configuration before saving it.
- Keep secrets out of frontend-readable settings.
- Record sensitive administrative changes where appropriate.

Do not create a general-purpose client-accessible mechanism for elevating roles.

N. Remaining modules

Inspect all other discovered modules, including coupons, newsletter subscriptions, media library, templates, customer support, or additional business features.

Do not skip a module simply because it is not listed above.

